INT. OBSERVATION CAR, MOUNTAIN SCENIC RAILWAY - SUNSET

A glass-domed car, brass fittings polished to honey. Plush velvet seats. Outside, peaks go violet under a rose-colored sky.

A WOMAN sits alone by the window, a travel journal on her knee. Mid-thirties, silk scarf, auburn hair pinned with a tortoiseshell comb. She writes, and stops, and watches the mountains.

A STRANGER enters from the vestibule: a tall man, forties, tweed jacket, a camera on a strap. He pauses at her row.

THE STRANGER
Is this seat taken?

WOMAN
There are forty empty seats.

THE STRANGER
I know. I counted.

She almost smiles. He sits across the aisle, not beside. Lifts the camera, lowers it again.

THE STRANGER (CONT'D)
The light's gone before I can catch it. Always.

WOMAN
Then stop catching it.

THE STRANGER
And do what?

WOMAN
Look at it.

He looks. A long beat. The train curves, and the whole western sky tilts into the car.

THE STRANGER
My name is Anselm.

ANSELM
(correcting himself)
Anselm Whitcombe-Diallo.

WOMAN
Philippa Okoro-Strand.

ANSELM
Philippa. That's a good mountain name.

PHILIPPA
Is there such a thing?

ANSELM
There is now.

The train rocks gently through a tunnel and out again.

PHILIPPA
Do you travel often?

ANSELM
Only when I've lost something.

PHILIPPA
What did you lose?

ANSELM
I'm hoping the mountains will tell me.

PHILIPPA
They don't talk.

ANSELM
They don't need to.

She closes her journal. The sky goes from rose to lavender. Neither of them speaks, and neither looks away.
